WEBVTT

NOTE Arvind Karunakaran, workshop at Stanford GSB (Nov 9, 2026), highlights. Captions from YouTube's automatic transcript, cleaned; review before publishing.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.580
So start with the workflow, right?

2
00:00:01.580 --> 00:00:03.500
Start with what is the
workflow, or workflows,

3
00:00:03.500 --> 00:00:07.580
two or three, that you're
really trying to re-engineer,

4
00:00:07.580 --> 00:00:08.940
because that is where the value is.

5
00:00:08.940 --> 00:00:12.220
The workflow touches on
different roles and functions.

6
00:00:12.220 --> 00:00:15.980
So if you identify, this is a workflow
that I'm really going to make better,

7
00:00:15.980 --> 00:00:19.660
and your product is in service
of making that workflow better,

8
00:00:19.660 --> 00:00:22.460
then you'll have a lot more
clarity on what needs to change:

9
00:00:22.460 --> 00:00:25.740
what incentives, what
job roles need to change.

10
00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:29.180
So I would think almost like
a workflow mindset, right?

11
00:00:29.180 --> 00:00:31.110
And having your own playbooks around that.

12
00:00:31.444 --> 00:00:37.684
So if the transformation unit is
only constituted by executives

13
00:00:37.684 --> 00:00:42.244
and technologists, like AI experts,
then it is not going well,

14
00:00:42.244 --> 00:00:44.644
because they don't really
even have visibility into

15
00:00:44.644 --> 00:00:48.484
what workflows they try to optimize,
what job roles are implicated.

16
00:00:48.484 --> 00:00:52.004
But if the transformation team
is very multidisciplinary,

17
00:00:52.004 --> 00:00:54.404
you have domain experts, your end users,

18
00:00:54.404 --> 00:00:59.284
at least one or two representatives from
each role implicated in that workflow.

19
00:00:59.284 --> 00:01:01.444
And more importantly,
what we have found is

20
00:01:01.444 --> 00:01:06.004
if the transformation team, or the center
of excellence, has work design experts,

21
00:01:06.004 --> 00:01:09.124
think of your Six Sigma
black belt type of people,

22
00:01:09.124 --> 00:01:11.044
not process-obsessed people, right?

23
00:01:11.044 --> 00:01:13.524
Because that's also
another warning, right?

24
00:01:13.524 --> 00:01:15.604
If they're too process-obsessed,
that's not great.

25
00:01:15.604 --> 00:01:18.564
But they should have an
inclination towards understanding

26
00:01:18.564 --> 00:01:21.844
work and workflow as a system,

27
00:01:21.844 --> 00:01:26.004
and how do you go about redesigning the
system, redesigning the work itself?

28
00:01:26.004 --> 00:01:27.204
That makes a huge difference.

29
00:01:27.204 --> 00:01:30.164
They think of it from that
workflow redesign perspective

30
00:01:30.164 --> 00:01:34.084
rather than just a technology
adoption, or this build versus buy,

31
00:01:34.084 --> 00:01:37.524
a classic CIO, IT department
perspective, right?

32
00:01:37.524 --> 00:01:41.114
Like, a work redesign
perspective. So that works.

33
00:01:41.164 --> 00:01:45.644
So the idea of a bundle is: if you
think of a job as a bundle of tasks,

34
00:01:45.644 --> 00:01:48.844
there is a glue that holds
the different tasks together.

35
00:01:48.844 --> 00:01:51.644
Say if you're a coder,
there are certain tasks you do.

36
00:01:51.644 --> 00:01:54.524
If you're an M&A lawyer,
there are certain tasks you do.

37
00:01:54.524 --> 00:01:57.164
If you're a radiologist,
you do certain tasks.

38
00:01:57.164 --> 00:01:59.804
Some of those jobs,
the glue is very strong.

39
00:01:59.804 --> 00:02:03.964
In other words, you have to be good
at task A to be good at task B,

40
00:02:03.964 --> 00:02:08.124
to be good at task C, even if AI
is getting better at task A, right?

41
00:02:08.124 --> 00:02:13.244
So that's an idea of a strong bundle.
In other words, the glue is very strong.

42
00:02:13.244 --> 00:02:15.884
Whereas there are certain other
jobs where the glue is very weak.

43
00:02:15.884 --> 00:02:18.764
There are only three or four tasks
that particular individual does.

44
00:02:18.764 --> 00:02:21.724
The tasks are loosely related.
You don't need to be good at task A

45
00:02:21.724 --> 00:02:23.164
to be good at task B, right?

46
00:02:23.164 --> 00:02:27.084
So what would be the examples?
Like a radiologist.

47
00:02:27.084 --> 00:02:30.844
Remember Geoff Hinton's
famous prediction in 2016

48
00:02:30.844 --> 00:02:34.444
that you don't need to train radiologists
anymore because AI is getting better.

49
00:02:34.444 --> 00:02:37.084
Not only did it turn out not to be true,

50
00:02:37.084 --> 00:02:40.364
or at least one part of
his prediction came true.

51
00:02:40.364 --> 00:02:44.124
The AI capabilities improved
in interpreting these scans,

52
00:02:44.124 --> 00:02:47.324
but the other part of the message,
that you don't need to train radiologists,

53
00:02:47.324 --> 00:02:51.664
or there's going to be less demand
for them, did not come out to be true.

54
00:02:51.701 --> 00:02:56.151
The most important reason is the job
of a radiologist is a strong one.

55
00:02:56.151 --> 00:02:58.951
In other words,
you had to do at least some

56
00:02:58.951 --> 00:03:03.191
of the interpreting of the scans
yourself to do your other tasks:

57
00:03:03.191 --> 00:03:06.711
talk to the attendings, train the
residents, talk to the patients.

58
00:03:06.711 --> 00:03:10.951
So if you try to unbundle a
job that has a strong glue,

59
00:03:10.951 --> 00:03:14.151
we have found time and again through
empirical research that it's not going well.

60
00:03:14.151 --> 00:03:16.481
The performance of that
person suffers, right?

61
00:03:16.501 --> 00:03:19.691
Think of a job as a
medical transcriptionist

62
00:03:19.691 --> 00:03:22.171
or a tier-one customer support agent.

63
00:03:22.171 --> 00:03:24.491
That's a job where the
glue is weak, right?

64
00:03:24.491 --> 00:03:27.131
They do some tasks.
They're loosely related.

65
00:03:27.131 --> 00:03:31.371
Those types of tasks you can really
redesign, right, between human and AI.

66
00:03:31.371 --> 00:03:34.251
You can completely rethink,
reimagine those jobs,

67
00:03:34.251 --> 00:03:36.401
sometimes consolidate two roles into one.

68
00:03:36.525 --> 00:03:40.925
You have to think about how do you
do the chaining between human and AI.

69
00:03:40.925 --> 00:03:44.765
Think of it as a speed breaker
on a street, right? On a highway,

70
00:03:44.765 --> 00:03:47.645
if you have a speed breaker every
10 meters, it's not helpful.

71
00:03:47.645 --> 00:03:49.885
You're really in a bumpy ride.

72
00:03:49.885 --> 00:03:54.365
But if you have a speed breaker every
300 miles, that's also not useful, right?

73
00:03:54.365 --> 00:03:58.045
Here, the speed breaker is when should
the human intervene and verify, right?

74
00:03:58.045 --> 00:03:59.485
That's the speed breaker.

75
00:03:59.485 --> 00:04:03.005
So you had to architect that,
what we call a chain, right?

76
00:04:03.005 --> 00:04:07.325
Or think of it as a seam,
say in your clothes or your pants.

77
00:04:07.325 --> 00:04:09.005
How are you designing that seam?

78
00:04:09.005 --> 00:04:13.325
In other words, when should the human
intervene, verify, give feedback,

79
00:04:13.325 --> 00:04:15.005
and architect that loop?

80
00:04:15.005 --> 00:04:18.295
That matters too when you're
trying to redesign job roles.
